Here is a simple example of a Rust application that uses the ElevenLabs API to read the contents of the clipboard aloud using TTS (Text-to-Speech):
```rust
use elevenlabs_api::{ElevenLabs, Voice};
use clipboard::{ClipboardContext, Clipboard};
use std::env;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    // Get the API key from the environment variable
    let api_key = env::var("ELEVENLABS_API_KEY").expect("ELEVENLABS_API_KEY must be set");

    // Create an instance of the ElevenLabs API client
    let client = ElevenLabs::new(api_key);

    // Get the clipboard contents
    let mut context = ClipboardContext::new().expect("Failed to create clipboard context");
    let contents = context.get_contents().expect("Failed to get clipboard contents");

    // Create a new voice instance
    let voice = Voice::new("en-US", "female");

    // Set the voice and text to read
    let text = contents.trim();
    let voice_id = voice.id();

    // Create a new TTS request
    let request = client.create_tts_request(text, voice_id);

    // Send the request and get the response
    let response = client.send_tts_request(request).await;

    // Print the response
    println!("Response: {:?}", response);
}
```
This code uses the `elevenlabs_api` crate to interact with the ElevenLabs API, the `clipboard` crate to read the clipboard contents, and the `tokio` crate for asynchronous programming.

You'll need to add the following dependencies to your `Cargo.toml` file:
```toml
[dependencies]
elevenlabs_api = "0.1.0"
clipboard = "0.7.0"
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
```
You'll also need to set the `ELEVENLABS_API_KEY` environment variable to your actual API key.

Note that this is just a simple example, and you may want to add error handling and other features to your application.

Also, you can use `reqwest` crate to make the HTTP request to the ElevenLabs API, instead of using the `elevenlabs_api` crate.

