import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;

public class User {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    // getters and setters
}

public class UserDTO {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    // getters and setters
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        User user = new User();
        user.setName("John");
        user.setAge(30);

        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
        UserDTO userDTO = mapper.convertValue(user, UserDTO.class);

        System.out.println(userDTO.getName()); // prints: John
        System.out.println(userDTO.getAge()); // prints: 30
    }
}
```

In this example, we create an instance of `User` and set its properties. Then we use `ObjectMapper` to convert the `User` object to a `UserDTO` object. The `convertValue` method takes the source object and the target class as arguments and returns an instance of the target class.

Note that the target class (`UserDTO`) must have a no-arg constructor, and the properties of the target class must match the properties of the source object.

Alternatively, you can use Lombok library to automatically generate getters and setters for your DTO class, and then use Jackson to convert the object to DTO.

```java
import lombok.Data;

@Data
public class UserDTO {
    private String name;
    private int age;
}
```

